  "narrative": {
    "text": "A comparison of four types of rapid review — a traditional systematic review conducted rapidly, a rapid review of systematic reviews, a rapid review of systematic reviews plus primary studies, and a rapid review of primary studies only — across twelve methodological dimensions. The traditional review allows up to 16 weeks where the three rapid forms allow up to 12. It differs from them throughout: no restrictions on literature search or number of databases against restrictions and 2–3 databases; complete data-extraction verification against one reviewer with selected verification; outcomes restricted to four or fewer against two to four with more if available. Rows also cover question types, number of questions, use of systematic reviews, grey literature, screening, study designs included, risk-of-bias assessment, and assessment of the quality of the body of evidence.",
